The information available on the web about the instakill artifact spoon
Houchou is not very plentyful, to say the least, and also not completely
correct, as it appears.  I am playing Version 0.0.7E6F3, which obviously
is not the most recent one, but I doubt that things have changed that
much since.

The spoilers say that Houchou instakills any monster you throw it at and
gets destroyed in the process.

I had an explore mode game running in which I got Houchou by
sacrificing, so I could make some experiments (and restart the saved
version to get a used-up Houchou back).

Trying to get rid of an annoying Ki-rin by throwing Houchou at it, I
found out that the spoon hit it (and disappeared) but did not destroy
the Ki-rin.  Later the same thing happened with an angel, but this time
I paid more attention. Houchou "does not seem to harm the angel".  So if
the spoon is at +0, it may hit and hence disappear but will not kill
monsters for which you need enchanted weapons. Hence a restriction to
the instakill rule.

Then I decided to enchant it.  I went to +3, since I believe any
artifact at +3 will do damage to all high-level monsters, in principle. 
Tried it for an angel (on the astral plane).  No problem, the angel is
dead.  I move to its original position and what do I see: a +3 Houchou! 
So if it is enchanted, it does not necessarily disappear, it is
reusable! (I don't think it was blessed, as it got cursed soon and then
disappeared after the next instakill.)  I killed several monsters,
including Famine and Death.  With Death, Houchou disappeared, but by
that time it had become cursed, which may have been the reason.

So, Houchou is a much more powerful and useful artifact than it appears
at first sight.  It is also more dangerous, as a monster might pick it
up, after the PC killed some big nasty with it.  The only rescue against
a Houchou thrown at the PC (that actually hits) would be an amulet of
life saving, I suppose.

